WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.557
Up front or just in time.

2
00:00:01.837 --> 00:00:04.632
There are two ways to get information into the context.

3
00:00:04.912 --> 00:00:12.037
Up front: retrieve or paste everything that might be relevant before the model starts, the classic RAG pattern from Studio 2.

4
00:00:12.317 --> 00:00:21.512
Just in time: give the agent lightweight references (file paths, document ids, queries) and tools to load content when it decides it needs it.

5
00:00:21.792 --> 00:00:36.811
Anthropic’s context engineering post argues that agents increasingly benefit from just-in-time retrieval, because the agent can follow the trail, use metadata such as file names and folder structure as clues, and keep only what matters.

6
00:00:37.091 --> 00:00:50.445
Their Agent SDK post recommends starting with agentic search (letting the agent use tools like grep over files) and adding semantic search only when you need the speed or the variety of queries it handles.

7
00:00:50.725 --> 00:00:56.272
The trade-off is real: runtime exploration is slower, and it only works with good tools.

8
00:00:56.552 --> 00:01:01.117
An agent with a bad search tool wastes its budget opening the wrong documents.

9
00:01:01.397 --> 00:01:03.339
Most real systems are hybrid.

10
00:01:03.619 --> 00:01:07.523
Claude Code is the standard example in the post: it loads CLAUDE.

11
00:01:07.803 --> 00:01:14.032
md files into context at the start, then uses glob and grep to find everything else as it works.

12
00:01:14.312 --> 00:01:16.744
You can apply the same split to your own agents.

13
00:01:17.024 --> 00:01:23.424
Put in up front the small, stable, always-relevant material (the task, the rules, a map of what exists).

14
00:01:23.704 --> 00:01:32.600
Leave the large, variable material (document bodies, logs, query results) behind tools that return small, paginated results.

15
00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:37.019
The post’s general advice fits here too: do the simplest thing that works.

16
00:01:37.299 --> 00:01:41.971
If your corpus is twenty pages and fits comfortably, paste it in and skip the tools.
